<urn:atm:triage:finding/RBQA-AW2-F001>
  a triage:Finding ;
  triage:findingId "RBQA-AW2-F001" ;
  triage:title "SqliteDiagnosticTimeline::open opened a private rusqlite Connection instead of routing through SharedDb's single writer" ;
  triage:description "qa-pr1179-r1 (ruthless-boundary-qa, confirmed by quality-mgr) at e61bb3588: SqliteDiagnosticTimeline::open() created its own rusqlite Connection against the same DB file; inert (not wired into any call path) but sprint-AW.2 deliverable 3 requires no second SQLite connection. arch-ctm's 8be5ac880 (feat(diagnostics): route timeline batches through writer lane) reports reusing the backend writer; qa-pr1179-r2 verifies every path (open/record_batch/query/prune) and the hot-path gate on writer/mod.rs. CLOSURE (qa-pr1179-r4, quality-mgr): corrected from an earlier overclaim -- prune() routes through the writer lane (WriteOp::PruneDiagnostics, see AW2-QA-B1 closure), but query() (diagnostic_timeline.rs ~89-119) routes through SharedDb::with_connection -> control_path_pool, the codebase's existing shared bounded read-connection pool used by other read paths, NOT a private ad hoc Connection. Ruling: this satisfies the finding's actual concern (no private connection bypassing established pooling/lane discipline); the ADR-ATM-RUSQLITE-002 'no competing writer connection' restriction applies to writes, not reads, so control_path_pool use for a read-only query() is architecturally sound and not a new violation. req-qa's round-4 finding (ATM-QA-AW2R4-002) correctly flagged that the closure text must not claim control_path_pool is unused outright -- this description is amended accordingly. Finding closed." ;
  triage:phaseId "phase-aw" ;
  triage:triageMode "initial_pass" ;
  triage:category "storage-boundary" ;
